// cursor.mn — Mentl's projection of the graph for a human at a position
//
// Hμ.cursor handle. Phase μ — Mentl active-surface composition. The
// opening handle of the kernel-closure follow-on; composes the closed
// kernel, invents nothing.
//
// Cursor is attention, not position. The text-caret is one weighted
// input to argmax; the gradient's global argmax over the live graph
// is the cursor proper. `??` is the developer's override that pins
// cursor to a chosen slot.
//
// The authority is PLAN §2: every subsystem is the one cursor-read in
// a different mode, and the felt surface is that read projected for a
// human.
//
// Composition tally — every `perform` resolves to substrate that
// already exists at handle landing time:
//
//   graph_chase           — types.mn:703 (GraphRead effect)
//   teach_gradient        — mentl.mn:78 (Teach effect, mentl_default arm)
//   teach_why             — mentl.mn:79 (Teach effect, mentl_default arm)
//   synth_propose         — mentl.mn:90 (Synth effect, OneShot stub today;
//                                         Hμ.synth-proposer fills the
//                                         MultiShot semantics post-H7)
//   verify_debt           — types.mn:807 (Verify effect)
//   mentl_context_at      — kind projection (this file)
//   pipe_context_of_handle — kind projection (this file)
//   ownership_of_kind     — kind projection (this file)
//   row_of_kind           — kind projection (this file)
//   verify_pending_at     — ledger projection (this file)
//
// Drift-mode audit per the cursor walkthrough §11: all nine PASS.
// Drift 1 (vtable):   no dispatch table; named perform calls only.
// Drift 4 (MTL):      one handler, eight reads, one composed record.
// Drift 5 (split state): caret + argmax fold into one Cursor value.
// Drift 7 (parallel): CursorView is one record (types.mn:354).
// Drift 8 (string flag): SuggestionKind is the tagged ADT.
// Drift 9 (deferred): every read resolves to live substrate; peer
//                     handles named (Hμ.cursor.transport, Hμ.synth-
//                     proposer, Hμ.cursor.cache, Hμ.gradient-delta).
import types

import effects

import format

import graph

import infer

import mentl

import own

import query

import verify

// ─── The Cursor effect ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Three ops. Each cursor_default arm body invokes resume exactly
// once on the projection, so the medium infers cardinality `OneShot`
// for all three — the cursor projects, it never captures or replays.
// MultiShot exploration lives in Synth (the tentacle Cursor calls
// into); Cursor itself is a one-call read.
//
// `with !Mutate` on cursor_default proves at compile time that the
// projection cannot corrupt oracle state — exactly the constraint
// PLAN §2's cached cursor locks for "surfaces query, never write."
effect CursorRead {
  // the by-HANDLE projection — every caller holds the node it means; a span
  // is not an address (every module numbers its lines from 1), and the
  // span-addressed op this replaced resolved the session's own cursor
  // through a module-blind scan
  cursor_at_handle(Handle) -> CursorView
  cursor_argmax(Caret) -> Cursor
}

// ─── The handler ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Per the cursor walkthrough §4: cursor_default composes the eight tentacle
// reads into one CursorView per arm. mentl_default surfaces the
// individual Teach ops; cursor_default is the same projection at a
// higher abstraction — "give me everything Mentl knows about this
// position, in one record."
//
// Both can coexist in the same `~>` chain — installing cursor_default
// AFTER mentl_default lets cursor_default's perform calls bubble
// `teach_gradient` / `teach_why` to mentl_default.
handler cursor_default {
  cursor_at_handle(handle) => // The eight kernel-primitive aspects fan out via `<|` — one position
  // borrowed (read-only) to eight branches, each branch projects one
  // tentacle's lens, output is the 8-tuple in primitive-order. The
  // shape on the page IS the cursor's read-tensor at this handle (PLAN
  // §2's cursor table: each tentacle is the same graph projected through
  // one kernel primitive). Resolution is the caller's: the address
  // transport resolves once by its line rule, the session holds its
  // cursor's handle, and re-resolving either here is the re-derivation.
  resume(cursor_view_of(handle)),
  cursor_argmax(caret) => resume(cursor_argmax_compute(caret)),
}

// ─── Multi-cursor projection — map over a position list ─────────────
//
// The DELETED cursor_pair_at (binary special-case) was erased per
// CLAUDE.md Anchor 6: pair-forms ARE the tell that an N-arity
// peer is missing. Multi-cursor projection IS
// `handles |> map(cursor_at_handle)` directly at the call site, the
// op invoked as a BARE call (`perform` dissolved, SYNTAX.md). The
// shape on the page IS the topology — the cursor reading N positions.
// N cores reading simultaneously is a `><` fanout under its own
// `~> Thread` at the call site — the schedule is read at the fanout's
// install, never across a call (Hβ.lower.schedule-specialized-callee).
// Drift 6 erasure: small-arity special-case removed.
//
// ─── IC fixpoint — `<~` feedback closure on the cursor ────────────────
//
// Build IS the `<~` loop closing on stability per
// PLAN §2's cached cursor. The cursor projects; accepted candidates
// fold back into the graph (GraphWrite under the IC handler);
// re-projection triggers per IC's broader-input invalidation; the
// loop converges when no Reason changes between cycles.
//
// The RHS is a FeedbackSpec — accumulate(initial_caret) starts at the
// caret and IC-narrows toward the gradient's argmax. Convergence test:
// reason-equality between cycles.
//
// `<~` does NOT require an ambient iterative context. The clock is
// INFERRED, never demanded: a `<~` site is a per-site line whose previous
// iteration is the enclosing fn's NEXT CALL (SYNTAX §feedback). The
// causality rule that is armed is E_ZeroDelayFeedback: `<~ delay(0)` has
// no prior to read.
//
// The eight-aspect projection runs per cycle — `<|` IS what's repeated
// inside the `<~` loop. Multi-cursor mode threads through `><` per
// cycle. The full composition exhibits ALL FIVE verbs:
//   `|>` sequential through cursor_argmax_compute
//   `~>` handler-attach for the CursorRead mode in force
//   `<~` feedback closing on stability
//   `><` parallel multi-cursor (a position-list fanout under ~> Thread)
//   `<|` divergence into the eight aspects (inside cursor_at_handle)
//
// This IS what mentl does. Not "compile" as a separate phase — the
// edit IS the compile IS the projection IS the loop.
//
// THE CACHED CURSOR IS NOT BUILT HERE, and the removed sketch is why.
// A handler named ic_fixpoint_handler stood at this spot: a second CursorRead
// carrying a last_epoch state, whose prose said it "resumes the terminal cursor
// and STOPS re-projecting" on a stable epoch, and which was "installed via
// `~>` above". Both claims were false. It was installed nowhere, and BOTH
// of its branches called cursor_argmax_compute — so the state it carried
// changed no result and the memoization it named did not exist. Anything
// that installed it believing the prose would have got zero caching and
// thought IC was live, which is worse than an absence.
// Deleted 2026-09-04 at the orphan-claims facet's first run.
//
// The design fault it hides is the part worth keeping: an epoch alone
// cannot key this read. cursor_argmax takes a CARET, and moving the caret
// without editing leaves the graph epoch unchanged while the correct
// cursor differs — so a stable-epoch cache returns the wrong position the
// first time a human moves without typing. The key is (epoch, caret), and
// the build is the residue entry
// Hβ.cursor.cached-argmax-keyed-by-epoch-and-caret.
//
// ─── Argmax computation — pure transform on the live graph ──────────
//
// Per the cursor walkthrough §3: enumerate gradient positions; score each by
// `gates_unlocked × proximity`; argmax. No state; no learning; no
// tuning. The proximity decay table is a literal scope-distance
// classification.
//
// Today: O(N · K) where N = handles in the live graph, K = candidates
// per handle. Acceptable for small programs. The O(N) form reads the
// oracle queue as graph content persisted with the module image
// (Hβ.persist.module-image-cache) — same semantics, better latency,
// never a side-file.
fn cursor_argmax_compute(caret) with !Mutate = {
  // SYNTAX «THole»: `??` is the gradient's syntactic absence marker. An
  // unfilled hole blocks the executable itself (E_UnresolvedHole), so
  // absence categorically outranks every annotation tightening — holes
  // rank among themselves by caret proximity; the annotation lattice is
  // the no-hole gradient. A structural tier, never a magic weight.
  //
  // The positions are the CARET'S MODULE'S own, and the argmax is the head
  // of the one ranked field (`rank_positions`), the list `mentl <file>:0`
  // renders whole. Both halves were false until 2026-10-01: positions were
  // every hole and declaration in the link, ranked by a proximity that
  // compared bare line numbers across files, so `mentl edit main` focused
  // the prelude's `unwrap_or` and accepting it wrote into main.mn; and the
  // argmax broke ties toward the LOWEST handle while the field broke them
  // toward the highest, so the field's head was not the session's focus.
  //
  // drift-audit: ignore — holes reused twice (tier test + the tier itself); a `<|` reuse, not a `|>` chain
  let mh = caret_module(caret)
  let holes = enumerate_hole_positions(mh)
  let positions = if len(holes) > 0 { holes } else { enumerate_gradient_positions(mh) }
  argmax_or_default(rank_positions(positions, caret), caret)
}

// The module a caret stands in — the module node itself when the caret is
// at a file's start, the module a node was born in otherwise.
fn caret_module(caret) = match caret {
  Caret(h, _) => module_of_position(h),
}

fn caret_handle(caret) = match caret {
  Caret(h, _) => h,
}

// A position's module is ONE column read (`graph_module_of`, stamped at the
// mint); a module node is its own. A span cannot answer this: every module
// numbers its lines from 1.
fn module_of_position(h) = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NModule(_) => h,
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_) | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => graph_module_of(h),
}

// Every handle the module minted that the program still holds, in mint order
// — one word compare per node against the module stamped at its birth, and
// none of a statement an edit replaced.
fn module_handles(mh: Int) with !Mutate = match graph_snapshot() {
  Graph(next, _spans) => filter({ h => graph_module_of(h) == mh && graph_node_current(h) }, range(0, next)),
}

// Two mints can stand at one source position, and the latest is the live
// one. The identity is the span's START: a reshaped node changes its extent
// but not where it begins.
fn latest_generation(handles) with !Mutate = filter(
  (h) => !any((h2) => h2 > h && span_start_eq(graph_span_of(h2), graph_span_of(h)), handles),
  handles
)

// An AUTHORED hole: the parser mints NHole with a fresh nonzero id for
// every `??` (and for each syntax-error recovery hole — an absence to fix
// either way); the program weave's absent-default is NHole(0), never a
// position.
fn hole_gate(handle) with !Mutate = if authored_hole(handle) { 1 } else { 0 }

fn enumerate_hole_positions(mh) with !Mutate =
  latest_generation(filter(authored_hole, module_handles(mh)))

// A gradient position IS a fn declaration — the teach verb's own scope
// (map_fn_stmts feeds FnStmt handles). The kind gate runs BEFORE the
// teach read: the ungated walk asked teach_gradient about every cell in
// range(0, next) — virgin cells and interior mints included — and a
// junk handle's fabricated suggestion entered the field under whatever
// coordinates its garbage span landed on (measured: prelude prose
// rendered as the entry's own line 1 — the C1c-era virgin-walk residue,
// closed here by the chased-kind filter it named). The module filter runs
// FIRST, so the teach read is asked of the module's own declarations only,
// never of the prelude's hundreds.
fn enumerate_gradient_positions(mh) with !Mutate =
  latest_generation(filter(gradient_position, module_handles(mh)))

fn gradient_position(i) = match graph_node_body(i) {
  NStmt(s) => match s {
    FnStmt(_, _, _, _, _) => match teach_gradient(i) {
      TeachNone => false,
      TeachAdd(_) => true,
      TeachAsk(_) => true,
      TeachNeed(_, _, _) => true,
      TeachLift(_) => true,
    },
    LetStmt(_, _, _) | TypeDefStmt(_, _) | EffectDeclStmt(_, _, _) | HandlerDeclStmt(_, _, _, _, _, _)
    | HandlerChainStmt(_, _) | ExprStmt(_) | ImportStmt(_) | RefineStmt(_, _, _) | AliasStmt(_, _)
    | RowAliasStmt(_, _) => false,
  },
  NExpr(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_) | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => false,
}

// ─── The one ranked field ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE ONE ORDER over a module's positions: score descending, then source
// position, then handle. The argmax is its head (`argmax_or_default`) and
// the field is all of it (`mentl <file>:0`, main.mn), so the position the
// session focuses is the position the field lists first by construction.
// Per-position scoring is independent; a fan across cores is a `><` under
// its own `~> Thread` at this site (Hβ.lower.schedule-specialized-callee).
fn rank_positions(handles, caret) -> [(Float, Handle)] with !Mutate = {
  let reach = module_reach_of(caret_module(caret))
  handles |> map((h) => position_key(h, caret, reach)) |> sort |> map(key_entry)
}

// The sort key: the two gains negated so the ascending structural sort puts
// the highest first — debt before locks — then the position's own line and
// column.
fn position_key(h, caret, reach) -> (Float, Float, Int, Int, Handle) with !Mutate = match graph_span_of(h) {
  Span(sl, sc, _, _) => match position_score(h, caret, reach) {
    (debt, locks) => (0.0 - debt, 0.0 - locks, sl, sc, h),
  },
}

fn key_entry(k: (Float, Float, Int, Int, Handle)) -> (Float, Handle) = match k {
  (neg_debt, neg_locks, _, _, h) => (0.0 - neg_debt - neg_locks, h),
}

// The gradient's two gains at a position, each weighted by proximity: the
// proof debt its answer discharges or names (a NEED is debt the developer's
// next declaration pays), then the guarantees it locks. Debt sorts first —
// the unproven part of the module is where the next move is (D4; until then
// one count of capability labels ranked every position, so a `with Pure`
// claiming three optimizations nothing performs outranked the division that
// could trap). An AUTHORED hole gates once (filling it lifts the
// executable's own refusal, E_UnresolvedHole), so within the hole tier
// proximity alone ranks. Any other answer-less position stays at zero.
fn position_score(handle: Handle, caret, reach) -> (Float, Float) with !Mutate = {
  let prox = position_proximity(caret_handle(caret), handle, reach)
  match teach_gradient(handle) {
    TeachNone => (0.0, float_of_int(hole_gate(handle)) * prox),
    TeachAdd(s) => suggestion_gains(s, prox),
    TeachAsk(ss) => match ss {
      [] => (0.0, 0.0),
      [s, ..._] => suggestion_gains(s, prox),
    },
    TeachNeed(_, _, _) => (prox, 0.0),
    // a lift pays no debt and locks no guarantee: it is listed, at no gain
    TeachLift(_) => (0.0, 0.0),
  }
}

fn suggestion_gains(s, prox: Float) -> (Float, Float) = match s {
  AnnotationSuggestion(_, lev, _) => match lev {
    Leverage(l) => (float_of_int(l.discharged) * prox, float_of_int(len(l.stands)) * prox),
  },
}

// A ranked position as the cursor the session projects: its handle, its
// own span over its node's reason, its score.
fn position_cursor(h, score) = match graph_chase(h) {
  GNode(_, reason) => Cursor(h, Located(graph_span_of(h), reason), score),
}

// ─── Proximity — read off the graph, between two positions ──────────
//
// Per the cursor walkthrough §5.1: the weights are literal floats encoding the
// design assumption "the developer's attention has a gradient, but
// the gradient never reaches zero, because cross-module impact still
// matters." No learned weights; the decay table is the project residue.
//
// Both ends are HANDLES. Their modules are one column read each, the
// enclosing declaration is a containment test INSIDE one module (where
// spans share coordinates), and a cross-module pair is near when the
// first's module reaches the second's through import edges (`reach`, the
// first module's closure, computed once by the caller). Until 2026-10-01
// this decay took two SPANS, asked each one's module by containment over
// module spans that every file numbers from line 1, and so read a decl on
// line 1 of any file as the caret's own declaration.
fn position_proximity(a, b, reach) -> Float with !Mutate = if a == b {
  same_handle_weight()
} else {
  let ma = module_of_position(a)
  let mb = module_of_position(b)
  if ma == mb {
    if same_enclosing_decl(a, b) { same_decl_weight() } else { same_module_weight() }
  } else if handles_contain(reach, mb) {
    transitive_dep_weight()
  } else {
    cross_module_weight()
  }
}

fn same_enclosing_decl(a, b) with !Mutate = match enclosing_decl(a) {
  None => false,
  Some(da) => match enclosing_decl(b) {
    None => false,
    Some(db) => da == db,
  },
}

fn caret_span_from_reason(reason) = match reason {
  Located(sp, _) => sp,
  Declared(_) | Inferred(_) | Fresh(_) | OpConstraint(_, _, _) | VarLookup(_, _) | FnReturn(_, _)
  | FnParam(_, _, _) | MatchBranch(_, _) | ListElement(_) | IfBranch(_) | LetBinding(_, _)
  | Unified(_, _) | Instantiation(_, _) | Placeholder(_) | MissingVar(_) | Refinement(_, _)
  | InferredCallReturn(_, _) | InferredPipeResult(_, _) | DefaultReason(_, _) | Accepted(_, _, _)
  | EffectEnters(_, _, _) | LabelEnters(_, _, _) | ChainInstall(_, _) | StateBinding(_, _) => span_zero(),
}

// Proximity decay constants — literal floats, not learned. Per
// the cursor walkthrough §5.1 design assumption: cross-module weight floors at
// 0.2 because cross-module impact still matters; same-handle weight
// is 1.0 because the developer's attention IS at the position.
fn same_handle_weight() -> Float with Pure = 1.0

fn same_decl_weight() -> Float with Pure = 0.85

fn same_module_weight() -> Float with Pure = 0.7

fn transitive_dep_weight() -> Float with Pure = 0.4

fn cross_module_weight() -> Float with Pure = 0.2

// The ranked field's head, or — where nothing is left to rank — the caret
// itself at impact 0, "fully annotated": the same record either way.
fn argmax_or_default(ranked: [(Float, Handle)], caret) = match caret {
  Caret(handle, reason) => match ranked {
    [] => Cursor(handle, Located(caret_span_from_reason(reason), Inferred("fully annotated")), 0.0),
    [(score, h), ..._] => position_cursor(h, score),
  },
}

// ─── Local pure helpers — one read each from the graph ──────────────
//
// Each helper extracts one of the eight aspects from a graph node.
// Pure transforms for structure, effects for context (PLAN §2 — only
// inference writes). The structure is the GNode; the
// extraction is reproducible.
//
// The one projection body `cursor_at_handle` serves: the eight aspects of the
// one read (PLAN §2), one stage each, sent the node and its kind by one `<|`.
fn cursor_view_of(handle) = match graph_chase(handle) {
  GNode(kind, _reason) => {
    let pos = (handle, kind)
    // In order: the graph and the environment (Query); the handlers
    // (Propose, Synth's multi-shot); the five verbs (Topology); the row
    // (Unlock); ownership as an effect (Trace); refinement (Verify); the
    // gradient (Teach); the inference and its reasons (Why).
    match pos <| (
      { (_, k) => k },
      { (h, _) => propose_at(h) },
      { (h, _) => verb_path_of(h) },
      { (h, k) => row_facet_at(h, k) },
      { (_, k) => ownership_of_kind(k) },
      { (h, _) => verify_pending_at(h) },
      { (h, _) => teach_gradient(h) },
      { (h, _) => teach_why(h) }
    ) {
      (query, propose, topology, row, trace, verify, teach, why) => CursorView(query, propose, topology, row, trace, verify, teach, why),
    }
  },
}

// The CARET-flavored span read (caret_span_from_reason, not the plain
// Located-or-zero of query.mn's span_of_handle) — renamed apart from it:
//
// An unbound node's type IS its own type variable — the chase
// terminated at this handle, so TVar(handle) is what HM proves.
fn ty_of_kind(handle, kind) = match kind {
  NBound(ty) => ty,
  NFree(_) => TVar(handle),
  NRowBound(_) => TVar(handle),
  NRecordRowBound(_, _) => TVar(handle),
  NRowFree(_) => TVar(handle),
  NErrorHole(_) => TVar(handle),
}

fn row_of_kind(kind) with Pure = match kind {
  NBound(ty) => extract_row_local(ty),
  NRowBound(r) => r,
  NFree(_) | NRowFree(_) | NRecordRowBound(_, _) | NErrorHole(_) => mk_ef_pure(),
}

fn extract_row_local(ty) with Pure = match ty {
  TFun(_, _, row) => row,
  TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _) | TRecord(_)
  | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => mk_ef_pure(),
}

// ownership_of_kind — derives Ownership from the graph's binding
// shape. NBound(TFun(...)) carries TParam list each with authored +
// resolved Ownership; for a node that IS a function, return the
// resolved ownership of its first param (the substrate-honest
// summary; full per-param ownership is in CursorView for handlers
// that want it). For non-function nodes, default Unmarked.
fn ownership_of_kind(kind) = match kind {
  NBound(ty) => ownership_of_ty(ty),
  NFree(_) | NRowBound(_) | NRowFree(_) | NRecordRowBound(_, _) | NErrorHole(_) => Unmarked,
}

fn ownership_of_ty(ty) = match ty {
  TFun(params, _, _) => if len(params) == 0 { Unmarked } else { param_resolved(list_index(params, 0)) },
  TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _) | TRecord(_)
  | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => Unmarked,
}

// The verbs that hold a node: the path from its declaration down to it, read
// along the tree's own edges, one frame for each verb site the path passes
// through, outermost first. It replaces a scan of every handle in the graph
// for a `PipeExpr` with the node as a child — which saw `|>`, `~>` and `<~`
// as one undifferentiated "inside pipe", never `><` at all, and never
// reached the render (measured on boot 8b071ba3: no Topology line at any
// caret).
fn verb_path_of(h) with !Mutate = {
  let path = decl_path_to(h)
  let held = fold([], verb_frame_at(path, ??, ??), range(0, len(path) - 1))
  match verb_shape_of(h, path) {
    Some(shape) => push(held, VerbFrame(h, VerbSite(shape))),
    None => held,
  }
}

// What the caret says of the extent an arena install opens, read off the
// roots the judgment wrote at the install (`ArenaRoots`): nothing it saw can
// root the extent, the stores that may, or that it could not tell. Nothing
// for an install that opens no arena, which has no cell.
fn extent_memory_of(h) with !Mutate = match graph_arena_roots_at(h) {
  RootsFree => Some("nothing the judgment sees can root the extent it opens, so its exit moves the result and reclaims the rest"),
  RootedAt(roots) => Some("stores at {join(", ", map(root_line, roots))} may root the extent it opens, which its exit journals"),
  RootsUnknown => Some("what may root the extent it opens is not known, so its exit keeps what it cannot read"),
  OpensNoArena => None,
}

fn root_line(root) with !Mutate = match root {
  (site, _) => match graph_span_of(site) {
    Span(line, _, _, _) => "line {int_to_str(line)}",
  },
}

// The verb at the node itself, with the fact it carries (`VerbShape`), so
// the caret on an operator says the shape in one sentence. A pipe inside a
// chain reports the whole chain and the stage it completes.
fn verb_shape_of(h, path) with !Mutate = if len(path) == 0 {
  None
} else {
  match graph_node_body(h) {
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PForward, _, _)) => {
      let elems = chain_elements(chain_head(path, len(path) - 1))
      Some(ChainShape(len(chain_elements(h)) - 1, len(elems) - 1, is_fanout(list_index(elems, 0))))
    },
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PFanout(FanShare), l, r)) => Some(ShareShape(plain_name(l), len(share_stages(r)))),
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PFanout(FanDistribute), _, _)) => Some(FanShape(2, schedule_of(installs_on(path)))),
    NExpr(FanoutExpr(branches)) => Some(fan_shape(h, branches, schedule_of(installs_on(path)))),
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PTee, _, r)) => Some(InstallShape(
      tee_handler_name(r),
      match tee_arms(r) {
        Some(arms) => handler_effect_names(arms),
        None => [],
      },
      extent_memory_of(h)
    )),
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PFeedback, _, r)) => Some(LoopShape(
      match feedback_depth(r) {
        DepthLiteral(n) => Some(n),
        DepthComputed => None,
        DepthUnstated => None,
      },
      line_owner(path)
    )),
    NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
    | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => None,
  }
}

// The head of the chain a pipe at path[i] belongs to: up the path while the
// pipe above holds this one as its left child.
fn chain_head(path, i) with !Mutate =
  if chain_continues_above(path, i) { chain_head(path, i - 1) } else { list_index(path, i) }

// A `><`'s shape as the judgment decided it (its boundary cell): one stage
// over every element of a list, or a branch per operand.
fn fan_shape(h, branches, s) with !Mutate = match graph_boundary_at(h) {
  SequenceFanout(_) => SeqFanShape(s),
  FanoutBoundary(_) | NoBoundary | InterpBoundary(_) => FanShape(len(branches), s),
}

fn is_fanout(h) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NExpr(FanoutExpr(_)) => true,
  NExpr(PipeExpr(PFanout(_), _, _)) => true,
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => false,
}

// A plain reference's name, so the shape can name the input a `<|` shares;
// None for any other expression.
fn plain_name(h) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NExpr(VarRef(name)) => Some(name),
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => None,
}

// The handlers the `~>` sites on a node's path install over it, outermost
// first: what a fanout's schedule is read from.
fn installs_on(path) with !Mutate = fold([], install_at(path, ??, ??), range(0, len(path) - 1))

fn install_at(path, acc, i) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(list_index(path, i)) {
  NExpr(PipeExpr(PTee, l, r)) => if l == list_index(path, i + 1) { push(acc, tee_arms(r)) } else { acc },
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => acc,
}

// The function whose record owns a `<~`'s line: the nearest declaration or
// function value above the site on its path.
fn line_owner(path) with !Mutate = match fold(None, owner_at(path, ??, ??), range(0, len(path) - 1)) {
  Some(name) => name,
  None => "its module",
}

fn owner_at(path, acc, i) with !Mutate = {
  let a = list_index(path, i)
  match graph_node_body(a) {
    NStmt(FnStmt(name, _, _, _, _)) => Some(name),
    NExpr(LambdaExpr(_, _)) => Some(match graph_span_of(a) {
      Span(line, _, _, _) => "the function value at line {int_to_str(line)}",
    }),
    NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
    | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => acc,
  }
}

// The path from a position's declaration down to it — what every upward
// question at a caret reads: the verbs that hold the node, the install that
// serves it, the function that encloses it and what the installs between
// them absorb. Empty at a position no declaration holds. It is the ancestry
// read up the parent edges and turned over; it was a search of the
// declaration's whole subtree, found by comparing the position's span with
// every declaration's in its module.
fn decl_path_to(h) with !Mutate = match ancestry(h, [h]) {
  Some(up) => reverse(up),
  None => [],
}

// Up from a node to the declaration its module holds — the node first and
// the declaration last. None for a module node and for a node no
// declaration's body reaches.
fn ancestry(h, up) with !Mutate = match graph_parent_of(h) {
  None => None,
  Some(p) => match graph_node_body(p) {
    NModule(_) => Some(up),
    NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_) | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => ancestry(p, push(up, p)),
  },
}

// The frame the site at path[i] contributes, read off which child the path
// takes next. A `|>` chain is one site however many pipes nest in it: an
// inner pipe of the chain contributes nothing, and the chain's head names
// the element the path enters.
fn verb_frame_at(path, frames, i) with !Mutate = {
  let site = list_index(path, i)
  match verb_role(site, path, i) {
    Some(role) => push(frames, VerbFrame(site, role)),
    None => frames,
  }
}

// The k-th operand of a `><` as the judgment decided the node: the list or
// the stage of a sequence fanout, or the k-th of n branches.
fn fan_role(site, k, n) with !Mutate = match graph_boundary_at(site) {
  SequenceFanout(_) => if k == 0 { SeqList } else { SeqStage },
  FanoutBoundary(_) | NoBoundary | InterpBoundary(_) => FanBranch(k, n),
}

fn verb_role(site, path, i) with !Mutate = {
  let next = list_index(path, i + 1)
  match graph_node_body(site) {
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PForward, _, _)) => if chain_continues_above(path, i) { None } else { chain_role(site, path, i + 1) },
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PTee, l, _)) => Some(if l == next { InstallBody } else { InstallHandler }),
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PFeedback, l, _)) => Some(if l == next { Recurrence } else { FeedbackDelay }),
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PFanout(_), l, r)) => if l == next { Some(FanInput) } else { shared_branch_role(r, path, i + 2) },
    NExpr(FanoutExpr(branches)) => match position_in(next, branches) {
      Some(k) => Some(fan_role(site, k, len(branches))),
      None => None,
    },
    NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
    | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => None,
  }
}

// A pipe that is the LEFT child of a `|>` above it is that chain's prefix.
fn chain_continues_above(path, i) with !Mutate = if i == 0 {
  false
} else {
  match graph_node_body(list_index(path, i - 1)) {
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PForward, l, _)) => l == list_index(path, i),
    NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
    | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => false,
  }
}

// A chain's elements are its left spine flattened — the source, then each
// stage in order — and the node's element is the first one the path enters.
// A path that ends on an inner pipe of the chain enters none: it IS a prefix.
fn chain_role(site, path, from) with !Mutate = {
  let elems = chain_elements(site)
  match fold(None, entered_element(elems, ??, ??), slice(path, from, len(path))) {
    Some(k) => Some(if k == 0 { ChainSource } else { ChainStage(k, len(elems) - 1) }),
    None => None,
  }
}

fn chain_elements(h) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NExpr(PipeExpr(PForward, l, r)) => push(chain_elements(l), r),
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => [h],
}

fn entered_element(elems, found, h) = match found {
  Some(_) => found,
  None => position_in(h, elems),
}

// A `<|` shares its input across the elements of the tuple on its right
// (`share_stages`), and the path enters the tuple before it enters a branch.
// A path that ends ON the tuple enters none.
fn shared_branch_role(r, path, at) with !Mutate = if at < len(path) {
  let stages = share_stages(r)
  match position_in(list_index(path, at), stages) {
    Some(k) => Some(ShareBranch(k, len(stages))),
    None => None,
  }
} else {
  None
}

// Which install serves an operation performed at a node — the Handler facet's
// answer at a perform, read up the path the topology reads: the innermost
// `~>` whose handler answers the op's effect. A function value minted on the
// way up runs where it is called, so the search stops there; an operation no
// install on the path answers leaves its declaration through the row.
type Served = ServedBy(Int, String) | ServedWhereCalled(Int) | ServedByCaller(Int)

// The op's effect and who serves it, when the node performs one: a call of
// an operation, or the operation's name at the caret. The search reads the
// node's ancestry, node first, and a perform no install on it answers is
// carried by the row of the declaration that ends it.
fn served_at(h) with !Mutate = match performed_effect(h) {
  None => None,
  Some(en) => match ancestry(h, [h]) {
    None => None,
    Some(up) => match fold(None, served_step(en, up, ??, ??), range(1, len(up))) {
      Some(s) => Some((en, s)),
      None => Some((en, ServedByCaller(last(up)))),
    },
  },
}

fn performed_effect(h) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NExpr(CallExpr(callee, _)) => ref_op_effect(callee),
  NExpr(VarRef(_)) => ref_op_effect(h),
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => None,
}

fn served_step(en: String, up, found, i) with !Mutate = match found {
  Some(_) => found,
  None => {
    let a = list_index(up, i)
    match graph_node_body(a) {
      NExpr(LambdaExpr(_, _)) => Some(ServedWhereCalled(a)),
      NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, _, _)) => if i + 1 < len(up) { Some(ServedWhereCalled(a)) } else { None },
      NExpr(PipeExpr(PTee, l, r)) => if l == list_index(up, i - 1) && tee_answers(r, en) {
        Some(ServedBy(a, tee_handler_name(r)))
      } else {
        None
      },
      NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
      | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => None,
    }
  },
}

// Whether the handler a tee installs answers the effect: its arms, read
// along the tee's edge to the declaration.
fn tee_answers(r, en: String) with !Mutate = match tee_arms(r) {
  Some(arms) => string_in_list(handler_effect_names(arms), en),
  None => false,
}

// The row facet spoken. A function value whose evaluation performs nothing
// — a reference to a declaration — says only what calling it performs.
fn show_row_facet(row) = match row {
  Evaluates(r) => show_said_row(r),
  WhenCalled(e, c) => if said_pure(e) {
    "{show_said_row(c)} when called"
  } else {
    "{show_said_row(e)}, and {show_said_row(c)} when called"
  },
  HoleRow => "whatever its position permits — a hole states no row",
}

fn said_pure(r) = match r {
  SaidClosed(row) => row_subsumes(row, mk_ef_pure()),
  SaidOpen(_, _) => false,
}

// What the facet's rows wait on, the evaluation's first.
fn facet_waits(row) = match row {
  Evaluates(r) => said_waits(r),
  WhenCalled(e, c) => said_waits(e) ++ said_waits(c),
  HoleRow => [],
}

fn said_waits(r) = match r {
  SaidClosed(_) => [],
  SaidOpen(_, waits) => waits,
}

// A row said to a person, its variables named in one extent. A closed row
// is written as the row algebra writes it. An open row names its effects,
// then what each free cell waits on, by the text of the values it names.
fn show_said_row(r) with !Mutate = said_text(r) ~> var_names

fn said_text(r) with !Mutate = match r {
  SaidClosed(row) => render_row(row),
  SaidOpen(named, waits) => {
    let lead = if len(named) == 0 { "" } else { "{render_row(mk_ef_closed(named))}, and " }
    "{lead}{waits_said(waits)}"
  },
}

// The cells an open row waits on, said: every cell no install masks in one
// phrase, then each masked cell in its own, with what the install answers.
fn waits_said(waits) with !Mutate = {
  let plain = waits |> filter(wait_unmasked) |> flat_map(wait_values)
  let lead = if len(plain) == 0 { [] } else { [whatever(plain, [])] }
  join(", and ", lead ++ (waits |> filter(wait_masked) |> map(masked_said)))
}

fn wait_cell(w: RowWait) = w.cell

fn wait_values(w: RowWait) = w.values

fn wait_mask(w: RowWait) = w.mask

fn wait_unmasked(w) = len(wait_mask(w)) == 0

fn wait_masked(w) = !wait_unmasked(w)

fn masked_said(w) with !Mutate = whatever(wait_values(w), wait_mask(w))

// One phrase of an open row: "whatever" and the values, each by its text in
// backticks and said once, then "except" and the effects an install between
// them and the position answers.
fn whatever(values, mask) with !Mutate = {
  let names = values |> map(value_text) |> unique |> map(quoted)
  let verb = if len(names) == 1 { "performs" } else { "perform" }
  let except = if len(mask) == 0 { "" } else { " except {said_list(map(render_eff_name, mask))}" }
  "whatever {said_list(names)} {verb}{except}"
}

// A value as its author writes it: the formatter's canonical text, whose
// names follow the reference edges, so a rename is said as renamed.
fn value_text(n) with !Mutate = render_body_tokens(n, graph_node_body(n))

fn quoted(s: String) = "`{s}`"

// A list said in prose: "a", "a and b", "a, b and c".
fn said_list(items) = if len(items) <= 1 {
  join("", items)
} else {
  "{join(", ", take(len(items) - 1, items))} and {last(items)}"
}

// The topology facet spoken, innermost verb first, each site placed by the
// caller's address form — the address projection and the terminal transport
// say it alike.
fn show_verb_path(frames, place) with !Mutate =
  join(", inside ", map(show_verb_frame(place, ??), reverse(frames)))

fn show_verb_frame(place, frame) with !Mutate = match frame {
  VerbFrame(site, role) => match role {
    VerbSite(shape) => shape_text(shape),
    ChainSource | ChainStage(_, _) | FanBranch(_, _) | SeqList | SeqStage | ShareBranch(_, _)
    | FanInput | InstallBody | InstallHandler | Recurrence | FeedbackDelay => "{verb_role_text(role)} at {place(graph_span_of(site))}",
  },
}

fn verb_role_text(role) = match role {
  ChainSource => "the source of the |>",
  ChainStage(i, n) => "stage {int_to_str(i)} of {int_to_str(n)} of the |>",
  FanBranch(i, n) => "branch {int_to_str(i + 1)} of {int_to_str(n)} of the ><",
  SeqList => "the list the >< applies its stage to",
  SeqStage => "the stage the >< applies to every element",
  ShareBranch(i, n) => "branch {int_to_str(i + 1)} of {int_to_str(n)} of the <|",
  FanInput => "the input the <| shares",
  InstallBody => "the body the ~> governs",
  InstallHandler => "the handler the ~> installs",
  Recurrence => "the recurrence the <~ feeds back",
  FeedbackDelay => "the delay the <~ declares",
  VerbSite(shape) => shape_text(shape),
}

// How a fanout's branches run, said once for both of its shapes.
fn schedule_clause(s) = if schedule_is_threaded(s) {
  "on threads under the schedule installed over it"
} else {
  "in sequence, since no schedule is installed over it"
}

// The verb's own sentence at its node, one a newcomer can say back, naming
// the fact the verb carries (SYNTAX §«Composing the five»).
fn shape_text(shape) = match shape {
  ShareShape(input, n) => {
    let what = match input {
      Some(name) => "`{name}`",
      None => "its input",
    }
    "the <| shares {what} with {shape_count(n, "branch", "branches")}, borrowed, so they cannot race"
  },
  FanShape(n, s) => "the >< runs {shape_count(n, "branch", "branches")}, each owning its input, {schedule_clause(s)}",
  SeqFanShape(s) => "the >< applies one stage to every element of the list on its left, each element a branch of its own, {schedule_clause(s)}",
  ChainShape(k, n, merges) => {
    let tail = if merges { ", and the fanout's product fills the first stage's open slots" } else { "" }
    if k == n {
      "the |> runs {shape_count(n, "stage", "stages")} in sequence{tail}"
    } else {
      "the |> runs {shape_count(n, "stage", "stages")} in sequence, this one completing stage {int_to_str(k)}{tail}"
    }
  },
  InstallShape(name, effs, memory) => {
    let base = if len(effs) == 0 {
      "the ~> installs `{name}` over everything to its left"
    } else {
      "the ~> installs `{name}` over everything to its left, answering {join(", ", effs)}"
    }
    match memory {
      Some(fact) => "{base}; {fact}",
      None => base,
    }
  },
  LoopShape(depth, owner) => match depth {
    Some(n) => "the <~ feeds back the output {shape_count(n, "tick", "ticks")} ago; its line is owned by `{owner}` and allocated once",
    None => "the <~ feeds back the previous output; its line is owned by `{owner}` and allocated once",
  },
}

// A count with its noun, the plural spelled rather than guessed.
fn shape_count(n, one: String, many: String) =
  if n == 1 { "1 {one}" } else { "{int_to_str(n)} {many}" }

fn position_in(h, hs) = match find({ (_, x) => x == h }, enumerate(hs)) {
  Some((k, _)) => Some(k),
  None => None,
}

// 4. What running the node performs: its evaluation, the fold over its
// subtree's row notes (`row_of_subtree`), and for a function value also the
// row its type carries, which calling it performs. A node typed as a
// function is not always one to call: `let f = () => log(2)` is typed as its
// value, and evaluating it mints the closure — `Memory + Alloc`, the fact a
// `!Alloc` judges — which the first form hid behind `Log when called`. The
// facet used to read the type's row for every node, which said nothing for
// an expression (`100 / (k - 1)` showed no row while it charges `Trap`) and
// was suppressed whenever it was empty.
fn row_facet_at(h, kind) with !Mutate = match row_of_subtree(h) {
  None => HoleRow,
  Some(eval) => match ty_of_kind(h, kind) {
    TFun(_, _, call) => {
      let called = said_at(h, call)
      WhenCalled(said_at(h, eval) |> said_without(said_waits(called) |> map(wait_cell)), called)
    },
    TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _) | TRecord(_)
    | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_) => Evaluates(said_at(h, eval)),
  },
}

// A function value's evaluation, less the cells its call row waits on: a
// `<|` applies its branch where the branch stands, so the branch's own
// notes charge what calling it performs, and the caret says that once, as
// what the value performs when called.
fn said_without(cells, r) with !Mutate = match r {
  SaidClosed(_) => r,
  SaidOpen(named, waits) => {
    let kept = filter(wait_outside(cells, ??), waits)
    if len(kept) == 0 { SaidClosed(mk_ef_closed(named)) } else { SaidOpen(named, kept) }
  },
}

fn wait_outside(cells, w) = cell_absent(cells, wait_cell(w))

// A row at a position as the caret says it. A row with no free cell is
// closed. An open row keeps the effects it names and, for each free cell,
// the values that cell waits on: until a caller supplies them, the row there
// is whatever those values perform, and a variable's letter says none of it.
fn said_at(h, row) with !Mutate = match resolve_row(row) {
  EfRow(named, _, EtOpen(edges)) => SaidOpen(named, row_waits(h, edges |> fold([], rooted_edge))),
  EfRow(_, _, EtClosed) | EfRow(_, _, EtAll) => SaidClosed(row),
}

// Two edges that chase to one free cell are one thing the row waits on: the
// cell keeps what both paths subtract, as a row's tail does for one cell.
fn rooted_edge(acc, e) with !Mutate =
  tail_set_insert(acc, row_edge(graph_chase_handle(edge_cell(e)), edge_mask(e)))

// The free cells a row is open on.
fn open_cells(row) with !Mutate = match resolve_row(row) {
  EfRow(_, _, EtOpen(edges)) => edges |> fold([], rooted_edge) |> map(edge_cell),
  EfRow(_, _, EtClosed) | EfRow(_, _, EtAll) => [],
}

// What each free cell of an open row waits on, in the order the position's
// text first reaches it. A cell's values are where its row enters: the
// innermost node under the position whose own row notes charge the cell. A
// cell that nothing under the position charges is the row its own value's
// type says calling it performs, so it waits on that value.
fn row_waits(h, edges) with !Mutate = match row_entries(map(edge_cell, edges), h) {
  (_, found) => {
    let entries = found |> map(entry_keyed) |> sort |> map(keyed_carrier)
    let reached = entries |> map(entry_cell) |> unique
    let unreached = edges |> map(edge_cell) |> filter(cell_absent(reached, ??))
    reached ++ unreached |> map(wait_of(h, edges, entries, ??))
  },
}

fn entry_cell(entry) = match entry {
  (c, _) => c,
}

// An entry keyed by where its node stands, as the Why walk orders carriers:
// a block lists its final before its statements, and the sentence follows
// the text.
fn entry_keyed(entry) = match entry {
  (_, n) => (span_order_key(graph_span_of(n)), entry),
}

fn entry_for(h, c) = (c, h)

fn enters_cell(c, entry) = entry_cell(entry) == c

fn cell_in(cells, c) = any(same_cell(c, ??), cells)

fn cell_absent(cells, c) = !cell_in(cells, c)

fn same_cell(a, b) with Pure = a == b

fn edge_on(c, e) = edge_cell(e) == c

// The cells a subtree's row notes charge, and where each enters it: a node
// whose own notes charge a cell that no node below it charges.
fn row_entries(cells, h) with !Mutate = match fold(([], []), child_entries(cells, ??, ??), node_children(h)) {
  (below, entries) => {
    let charged = graph_rows_at(h) |> flat_map(open_cells) |> filter(cell_in(cells, ??))
    let entering = charged |> filter(cell_absent(below, ??)) |> unique
    (unique(below ++ charged), entries ++ map(entry_for(h, ??), entering))
  },
}

fn child_entries(cells, acc, child) with !Mutate = match acc {
  (below, entries) => match row_entries(cells, child) {
    (more, found) => (below ++ more, entries ++ found),
  },
}

fn wait_of(h, edges, entries, c) with !Mutate = {
  let values = entries |> filter(enters_cell(c, ??)) |> flat_map(entry_values(c, ??))
  let mask = match find(edge_on(c, ??), edges) {
    Some(e) => edge_mask(e),
    None => [],
  }
  RowWait{cell: c, mask, values: if len(values) == 0 { [h] } else { values }}
}

// The values whose row an entry charges. A call or a stage that is handed a
// function value whose call row is the cell waits on what it was handed: a
// call of `map` waits on the function it is handed, whose row `map` only
// passes on. Otherwise it waits on what the node applies: a call's callee, a
// pipe's stage, or the node itself, which is how a `<|` branch is its own
// stage.
fn entry_values(c, entry) with !Mutate = match entry {
  (_, n) => match graph_node_body(n) {
    NExpr(CallExpr(callee, args)) => handed_or(c, args, callee),
    NExpr(PipeExpr(PForward, piped, stage)) => if hands_cell(c, piped) { [piped] } else { stage_values(c, stage) },
    NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
    | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => [n],
  },
}

fn stage_values(c, stage) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(stage) {
  NExpr(CallExpr(_, args)) => handed_or(c, args, stage),
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => [stage],
}

fn handed_or(c, args, applied) with !Mutate = {
  let handed = filter(hands_cell(c, ??), args)
  if len(handed) == 0 { [applied] } else { handed }
}

// Whether a value is a function whose call row is open on the cell.
fn hands_cell(c, v) with !Mutate = match graph_chase(v) {
  GNode(NBound(TFun(_, _, row)), _) => cell_in(open_cells(row), c),
  GNode(_, _) => false,
}

// The Propose aspect: ONE read fills both the op's allowed row and the
// Context — the enclosing fn's AUTHORED declared row. The published scheme
// row is the MEET (the negation deliberately does not survive publication,
// PLAN §7), so the authored signed set at the FnStmt is the only live home
// of `!E` at a decl; the hole's own node row (the pure row for a refined-Int
// hole) was the wrong source entirely.
// Propose is the ABSENCE facet: enumeration fills a socket. A BOUND node's
// socket is filled, so its proposals are empty BY CONSTRUCTION — the render's
// silence rule made structural (SYNTAX §Gradient: "Propose only at holes";
// what fits a filled position is Teach's annotation question, not candidate
// enumeration). This also fences the first live float-position enumeration
// from the float-evidence call-convention gap (an f64-arg candidate ctor
// dispatched through an all-i32 $ft and trapped `indirect call type mismatch`
// the hour the cursor-address transport projected a Float node — the named
// peer Hβ.emit.float-evidence-ft; the hole-typed gates exercise the
// enumeration where it belongs).
// Propose is the ABSENCE facet: enumeration fills a socket, and absence is
// the AUTHORED-hole fact (authored_hole — body NHole with a real id and a
// real span), never the cell state (a typed hole's cell is BOUND — typed
// absence is the design) and never a bare body probe (the `??` mint carries
// two handles and an unregistered slot reads zero-fill). This is the same
// read the transport gates patches on — one truth, two readers. A filled
// node's proposals are empty BY CONSTRUCTION, which also fences the
// float-evidence call-convention gap (an f64-arg candidate ctor through an
// all-i32 $ft — the named peer Hβ.emit.float-evidence-ft, first fired by
// the first float-position projection ever taken).
fn propose_at(h) with !Mutate = if authored_hole(h) {
  let ctx = mentl_context_at(h)
  match ctx {
    Context(_, allowed, _, _) => synth_propose(allowed, ctx),
  }
} else {
  Proposal({rejected: [], verdict: VNone})
}

// Synth's Context reads LIVE from the graph the cursor sits in — never
// fabricated: scope bindings ARE the env snapshot (the same bindings
// infer wrote); the row is what the hole's position may perform
// (`allowed_row_at`); the enclosing declaration feeds the vocabulary's
// nontermination exclusion. At a top-level position with no enclosing
// fn, the node's own row stands and there is no enclosing declaration.
fn mentl_context_at(handle) with !Mutate = match env_snapshot() {
  Env(scope_bindings) => match graph_chase(handle) {
    GNode(kind, _) => {
      let path = decl_path_to(handle)
      match fold(None, innermost_fn(path, ??, ??), range(0, len(path) - 1)) {
        Some((effs, k)) => Context(scope_bindings, allowed_row_at(path, k, effs), Some(list_index(path, k)), handle),
        None => Context(scope_bindings, row_of_kind(kind), None, handle),
      }
    },
  },
}

// The function that encloses the position is the innermost one on its path:
// a nested `fn` encloses tighter than the declaration that holds it. It was
// found by scanning every handle in the graph for a function whose body
// contained the position, a walk of each candidate's subtree per handle.
fn innermost_fn(path, found, i) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(list_index(path, i)) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, effs, _)) => Some((effs, i)),
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => found,
}

// What a candidate at the hole may perform: what the enclosing declaration
// already performs — its inferred row, CLOSED, because the medium never
// silently widens a function's capabilities — or its authored row when it
// has one, and in both cases every effect a `~>` between the hole and the
// declaration's body absorbs, since the handler around the hole catches it
// and nothing outside sees it. The authored clause alone used to answer,
// so an undeclared fn allowed Pure and `fn main() -> Int = (??) ~> h`
// refused every candidate performing what `h` absorbs
// (tests/frontier/mn-hole-row-absorbed.mn, measured 2026-09-25).
fn allowed_row_at(path, k, effs) = {
  let absorbed = fold([], absorbed_step(path, ??, ??), range(k + 1, len(path) - 1))
  let base = if len(effs) == 0 {
    match graph_chase(list_index(path, k)) {
      GNode(kind, _) => match resolve_row(row_of_kind(kind)) {
        EfRow(present, _, _) => mk_ef_closed(present),
      },
    }
  } else {
    build_declared_row(effs)
  }
  union_row(base, mk_ef_closed(absorbed))
}

// The effects absorbed between the enclosing function and the hole: every
// `~>` the path enters by its body side contributes the names its handler's
// arms handle — the derivation `infer_pipe_tee` subtracts at the same edge.
// Each level used to re-walk its children's subtrees to find the one holding
// the hole, which the path already knows.
fn absorbed_step(path, acc, i) with !Mutate = match graph_node_body(list_index(path, i)) {
  NExpr(PipeExpr(PTee, left, right)) => if left == list_index(path, i + 1) {
    match tee_arms(right) {
      Some(arms) => name_set_union(acc, derive_handler_enames(arms, 0, [])),
      None => acc,
    }
  } else {
    acc
  },
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => acc,
}

// verify_pending_at — Verify ledger holds ALL pending refinement
// obligations as (Span, Predicate, Reason) rows — the one order
// `verify_ledger` keeps and CursorView's verify slot declares. Consumers want the subset at one
// handle, WHOLE: the Reason is the Why-walk's edge and the Span the
// render's anchor — the old map-to-Predicate discarded both (the
// converged judge caught the slot mismatch it left).
//
// The subset is the handle's MODULE's: an obligation carries the module it
// was raised in, and every module numbers its lines from 1, so a span alone
// matched a stranger's claim on the same lines — a declaration on lines 3–5
// of main.mn counted the pending claims on lines 3–5 of every module it
// linked (D4's audit; the class D3 closed for positions).
fn verify_pending_at(handle) with !Mutate =
  verify_debt() |> filter(pending_in(graph_module_of(handle), graph_span_of(handle), ??))

// An obligation is AT a node when its claim sits inside the node's extent —
// the node's own and its sub-expressions'. Lines used to be compared, so every
// node on a line answered the line's whole debt — the source of a `|>` chain
// counted the claim its second stage owes (measured on boot 8b071ba3).
fn pending_in(m, target, o) = match o {
  (dm, debt_span, _, _) => dm == m && span_contains(target, debt_span),
}
